Nevertheless, this structural and functional alignment must not obscure the profound ontological divergence that separates these frameworks, for they differ fundamentally in their accounts of what performs the disclosing and how disclosure relates to Being itself. In the Aristotelian schema, phantasia operates as a psychological faculty or power belonging to the soul, wherein the imagination serves as an intermediary capacity that processes sensory data and produces representations; disclosure here remains an epistemological function performed by a faculty within a being, directed toward making objects available for cognitive apprehension. By contrast, Erschlossenheit designates not a faculty or capacity but rather the very structure of Dasein's Being, whereby disclosure constitutes the ontological condition through which Dasein exists as that entity for whom Being is always already an issue; here, what performs the disclosing is not a power possessed by Dasein but the disclosedness that Dasein itself is, such that the relationship between disclosure and Being becomes internal rather than instrumental. [CITATION NEEDED].

The frameworks fundamentally diverge in their ontological commitments: Aristotle's hylomorphic faculty psychology treats phantasia as a distinct psychic capacity (dynamis) of the soul operating within a hierarchical structure, while Heidegger's existential analytic rejects substance ontology in favor of existential structures that constitute Dasein's Being. Aristotle's approach involves "a double type of consideration" regarding the soul (ψυχή), with the question being "what the task of the φυσικός, with regard to the ψυχή, genuinely is," situating phantasia within natural philosophy's investigation of psychic faculties as one power among others—sensation, imagination, intellection—each occupying a determinate position within the soul's functional architecture. This investigation proceeds from the fundamental recognition that "all affections of the soul appear to be with body" (τὰ τῆς ψυχῆς πάθη πάντα εἶναι μετὰ σώματος), grounding psychological capacities in hylomorphic substance wherein form and matter constitute an indissoluble unity that makes the soul the actuality of an organic body. Heidegger's framework, by contrast, operates through "the being whose being constitutes the τί ἤν εἶναι," reinterpreting Aristotelian essence (οὐσία) not as substance but as the particularity of beings in their existential constitution, whereby what-it-was-to-be-that-thing becomes not a fixed nature possessed by a subject but the temporal unfolding through which Dasein's Being is disclosed. These divergences reveal fundamentally different ontological projects: Aristotle operates within a framework where phantasia is one faculty among others in the soul's hierarchical organization, grounded in the hylomorphic unity of form and matter, whereas Heidegger dissolves faculty psychology into existential structures, rejecting the notion of Dasein as a substance with properties in favor of understanding Dasein as the clearing in which Being discloses itself—such that where Aristotle asks what capacities the soul possesses, Heidegger asks about the Being of the entity that can question Being. [CITATION NEEDED].

Despite these divergences, Heidegger's engagement with Aristotelian texts suggests he finds resources for his own project within the Greek tradition, even as he radically reinterprets them. The sustained attention Heidegger devotes to Aristotelian concepts throughout his career—particularly his lectures on Aristotle's Metaphysics Θ 1-3 and his repeated return to the notion of energeia—indicates that he perceives within the Greek philosophical vocabulary possibilities for articulating Being that subsequent metaphysical traditions have obscured. When Heidegger appropriates Aristotle's distinction between dynamis and energeia, he transforms it from a metaphysical account of potentiality and actuality into an ontological framework for understanding Being as temporal presencing, whereby the Greek experience of being-at-work (energeia) becomes a way of thinking presence that precedes the subject-object dichotomy of modern philosophy. This hermeneutic strategy reveals Heidegger's conviction that pre-Socratic and early Greek thought, including Aristotle's texts when read against their scholastic interpretations, preserve traces of a more originary encounter with Being—an encounter that Western metaphysics subsequently concealed through its progressive reification of Being as constant presence (ousia as beständige Anwesenheit). The radical reinterpretation Heidegger performs therefore operates not as rejection but as retrieval (Wiederholung), seeking to uncover within Aristotelian terminology resources for dismantling the very metaphysical tradition that claimed Aristotle as its foundation, such that the Greek texts become sites where forgotten possibilities for thinking Being remain latent, awaiting phenomenological excavation. [CITATION NEEDED].

Heidegger's phenomenological appropriation of Aristotelian concepts reveals a methodological transformation wherein Greek insights about disclosure are retrieved and reinterpreted through existential-ontological analysis, preserving the concern with unconcealment while rejecting traditional psychological frameworks that would reduce phantasia to mere mental representation. This retrieval operates through what Heidegger characterizes as resources that "are all at Dasein's disposal," whereby inherited interpretations and concepts become available for existential analysis rather than functioning as fixed doctrines binding phenomenological inquiry to scholastic categories. The concern with "uncovering" and the impossibility of "dissimulating" or "mis-leading" in primordial disclosure demonstrates Heidegger's retrieval of Greek aletheia (unconcealment) as central to his phenomenological method, such that Aristotelian phantasia becomes legible not as psychological faculty but as an early articulation of how beings manifest themselves prior to theoretical objectification. Scholarly engagement with "Phantasia in Aristotle's Explanation of Action" continues this interpretive dialogue, examining how Aristotelian psychology might relate to phenomenological disclosure when read against the grain of faculty psychology. Heidegger's engagement with Aristotle thereby exemplifies his method of phenomenological retrieval (Wiederholung), wherein Greek philosophical insights are not simply repeated but transformed through the lens of fundamental ontology, such that the concern with how beings show themselves—whether as phantasmata in Aristotelian psychology or as disclosed entities in Heideggerian phenomenology—persists across the frameworks even as the ontological ground shifts from substance to existence, revealing in Aristotle's phantasia a proto-phenomenological insight into disclosure that anticipates, however imperfectly, the existential analytic of Dasein. [CITATION NEEDED].

